aarch64: fix the poll() millisecond-to-timespec conversion on the ppoll path

arm64 Linux has no __NR_poll, so sys_poll_nocancel falls back to __NR_ppoll,
which takes a struct timespec. The ms -> timespec conversion had the two fields
swapped and mis-scaled:

    .tv_sec  = (timeout % 1000) * 1000000,
    .tv_nsec = timeout / 1000

so a 500 ms timeout became tv_sec = 500000000 (about 15.8 years) and a
sub-microsecond tv_nsec. Every poll() with a positive timeout waited
effectively forever on arm64; the x86 __NR_poll path passes the ms value
straight through and never hit this. Convert correctly: whole seconds into
tv_sec, the millisecond remainder into tv_nsec.

Signed-off-by: Niclas Overby <niclas@overby.me>

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/select/poll.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/select/poll.c
@@ -26,8 +26,8 @@
 		ret = LINUX_SYSCALL(__NR_poll, fds, nfds, timeout);
 	#else
 		struct ppoll_timespec timeout_ts = {
-			.tv_sec = (timeout % 1000) * 1000000,
-			.tv_nsec = timeout / 1000
+			.tv_sec = timeout / 1000,
+			.tv_nsec = (long)(timeout % 1000) * 1000000
 		};
 		
 		ret = LINUX_SYSCALL(__NR_ppoll, fds, nfds, (timeout < 0) ? NULL : &timeout_ts, NULL);
